With this sequence the time behaves correctly (60 → 61). So the earlier 170.15 must have come from a different interaction in func.js. Looking at func.js again:

```js
await page.click('#presets button:nth-child(2)');
await page.waitForTimeout(1600);
const luz = await page.evaluate(...);
ok(luz.exp > 1, ...);
await page.click('#freeBtn');
...
await page.click('#playB');
await page.waitForTimeout(1200);
const adv = await page.evaluate(() => window.__alhambra.tour.time);
```

Hmm, in func.js the order is: ... `await page.click('#freeBtn')` (free on), then check, then `await page.click('#freeBtn')` (free off), then `await page.click('#playB')`, wait 1200, then read the time → 170.15.

But in my dbg2 the same sequence gives 61.31. The difference: in func.js, before the free-mode steps there's the "bajo" evaluate block which sets `A.tour.time = t` for t from 0 to T_TOTAL in a loop!!!

```js
const bajo = await page.evaluate(() => {
    const A = window.__alhambra; const out = [];
    ...
    for (let i = 0; i <= 200; i++) {
      const t = i / 200 * A.T_TOTAL;
      A.tour.time = t;
    }
    return out;
});
```

YES! That leftover debug block sets tour.time to T_TOTAL. My own test caused it. 

So the app is fine. Let me remove that block from func.js and re-run to confirm all tests pass.

